# ==================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES
#   Git consults this file to decide which UNTRACKED paths to hide. It
#   has no effect on a path that is already tracked: once a file is in
#   the index Git keeps reporting it, whatever the rules here say. That
#   asymmetry is why the audit below passes --no-index. Without that
#   flag `git check-ignore` consults the index and answers "not
#   ignored" for a tracked file that a rule really would have hidden,
#   turning a genuine defect into a false pass.
#
#   One further quirk of that command decides how the audit must be
#   written. Plain `git check-ignore` lists only paths that really are
#   excluded, but `-v` reports the last pattern to match a path even
#   when that pattern is a `!` negation - so `-v` prints a line for
#   .env.example, which is NOT ignored. Use the plain form to sweep
#   (nothing should print) and `-v` only to ask why one specific path
#   resolved as it did.
#
# SCOPE AND PRECEDENCE
#   Git applies the LAST matching pattern, so a `!` negation must come
#   after the rule it re-includes. Every negation below is live - it
#   rescues a path that an earlier pattern in the same block genuinely
#   matches. There are no defensive negations for paths nothing
#   matches: a negation that rescues nothing is dead weight that later
#   reads as protection it does not provide.
#
#   A directory rule and a wildcard rule are not interchangeable.
#   `.vscode/` would stop Git descending into the directory at all, so
#   no `!` inside it could ever take effect; `.vscode/*` keeps the
#   descent and lets the negations work. The same care drives the
#   leading slash on /target/, /out/, /logs/ and /blitzy/: anchored to
#   the repository root, those rules cannot reach a same-named
#   directory deeper in the tree. Only the credential rules are left
#   unanchored on purpose, for the reason given beside them.
#
#   Two different tests decide whether a rule belongs here. A rule for
#   generated output must name something this project's own toolchain
#   demonstrably produces, or it is dead. A rule that prevents a
#   secret, editor or operating-system file from being committed is a
#   preventive control: its whole value is that it fires before the
#   accident, so it is retained even though nothing matches it today.
#
# WHAT MUST NEVER BE IGNORED
#   app/**            the frozen COBOL corpus - 148 tracked files that
#                     serve at once as the parity oracle, the
#                     field-contract source and the traceability anchor
#   samples/**        legacy z/OS build tooling, including two tracked
#                     binary runtime archives under samples/m2
#   diagrams/**       legacy architecture illustrations
#   src/**            Java sources, resources, Flyway migrations, the
#                     validation JSON and the test fixtures and
#                     parity baselines under src/test/resources
#   docs/**, DECISION_LOG.md, TRACEABILITY_MATRIX.md   evidence
#   observability/**, localstack-init/**, Dockerfile,
#   docker-compose.yml, .github/**, .mvn/wrapper/**, pom.xml, mvnw,
#   mvnw.cmd, .env.example                             deliverables
#
#   No glob below matches a content format this repository tracks -
#   there is no *.txt, *.json, *.sql, *.html or *.zip rule - and no
#   bare `build/` or `data/` either. Thirteen tracked .txt files are
#   fixed-width parity fixtures, three tracked .json files are
#   application resources, two tracked .zip files are the legacy
#   runtime archives, and `data` is a real path component of app/data,
#   so any of those patterns would silently hide shipped content. The
#   extension globs that do appear cover only build output, runtime
#   files, private key material and editor state, none of which this
#   repository has ever tracked.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED
#   Build reports. Every report this build emits already lands under
#   target/. pom.xml pins the JaCoCo execution data and HTML report and
#   the OWASP report to ${project.build.directory}; Surefire and
#   Failsafe default there; .github/workflows/build.yml uploads exactly
#   target/site/jacoco/**, target/carddemo-*.jar and
#   target/dependency-check/**. Restating those paths at the root would
#   duplicate one rule and could only drift out of step with it.
#
#   Container state. docker-compose.yml declares NAMED volumes -
#   postgres-data, localstack-data, prometheus-data, grafana-data - so
#   Docker holds that data outside the repository, and its only bind
#   mounts are read-only mounts of tracked files INTO the containers.
#   No compose run writes into the working tree.
#
#   A wrapper jar. .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties sets
#   distributionType=only-script, so no maven-wrapper.jar is ever
#   fetched into .mvn/wrapper and no rule is needed to hide one.
#
#   Other language stacks. This is a single-module Maven project with
#   no Gradle, Node, Terraform, Kubernetes or front-end build, and the
#   AWS CLI virtualenv the setup guide provisions lives outside the
#   repository. Personal tooling is environment-specific and belongs in
#   a global excludes file (`git config core.excludesFile`), not here.
#
# HOW TO VERIFY A CHANGE TO THIS FILE
#   git ls-files | git check-ignore --stdin --no-index
#                                        the sweep; MUST print nothing
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v -- PATH   which rule matched a path
#   git check-ignore --no-index -q -- PATH   exit 0 ignored, 1 visible
#   git status --porcelain --ignored=matching   what is hidden right now
#   git diff -- app samples diagrams            MUST stay empty
#
# FAILURE MODES TO AVOID
#   Never add a glob for a format this repository tracks, and never add
#   a bare directory name that can match at arbitrary depth. `data/`
#   would hide app/data and `*.zip` would hide the two runtime archives
#   under samples/m2. Both failures are silent: Git reports nothing,
#   the files simply stop reaching a fresh clone. Re-run the --no-index
#   sweep above after any edit to this file - it is the only check that
#   catches this class of mistake.
# ==================================================================

# ---- Maven build output. This is the one build directory, and it
# ---- covers the packaged jar, the compiled classes and every
# ---- generated report. Anchored, because pom.xml declares no
# ---- <modules>: the build is single-module, so /target/ is complete,
# ---- and an unanchored target/ would additionally reach a directory
# ---- of that name anywhere below - src/test/resources/target/
# ---- included, which is protected fixture space. Should this ever
# ---- become a multi-module build, add one anchored rule per module
# ---- rather than dropping the leading slash.
/target/

# ---- A stray class file left beside a source by an ad-hoc `javac`
# ---- invocation, which lands outside target/ and is never wanted.
*.class

# ---- IntelliJ IDEA's own compiler output, written to the project root
# ---- when the IDE builds in place instead of delegating to Maven.
/out/

# ---- JVM fatal-error, replay and heap-dump files. The JVM writes
# ---- these into the working directory, so the build directory above
# ---- does not cover them. MAVEN_OPTS caps the heap at 1 GB on a small
# ---- host, which makes a heap dump a realistic outcome of a big run.
hs_err_pid*
replay_pid*
*.hprof

# ---- Application logs written by a local run. Anchored, because a
# ---- nested logs directory would belong to whatever produced it.
/logs/
*.log

# ---- Process-id file written by Spring Boot's ApplicationPidFileWriter.
*.pid

# ---- Local scratch: platform screenshots and screen recordings, and
# ---- the ad-hoc validation scripts that must never reach a commit.
/blitzy/
blitzy_adhoc_test_*

# ---- Local environment overrides. `.env.*` also matches the template
# ---- .env.example, which .env.example itself declares must stay
# ---- tracked ("Copy it to .env, which is git-ignored"), so the
# ---- negation that follows is required rather than defensive. The
# ---- JWT signing key is environment-indirected with no committed
# ---- default, so a developer's real values live only in .env.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---- Private key material and credential stores. Public certificate
# ---- formats are deliberately absent - *.crt, *.cer, *.pub and
# ---- *.truststore carry no private key, so a public test certificate
# ---- added later stays visible. id_rsa and id_ed25519 are matched
# ---- exactly, not as id_rsa*, so the .pub half is not hidden too.
# ---- These three are the one place where an unanchored directory or
# ---- basename rule is wanted: a leaked credential is a hazard at any
# ---- depth, so .aws/, secrets/ and credentials deliberately match
# ---- throughout the tree. No deliverable carries any of those names.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_ed25519
.aws/
credentials
secrets/

# ---- IntelliJ IDEA project state, all of it user-local here.
.idea/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws

# ---- VS Code. Only user-local state is hidden: the wildcard form is
# ---- required so Git still descends into the directory and the three
# ---- shareable team files below survive. settings.json stays ignored
# ---- because it carries per-developer preferences.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/tasks.json

# ---- Eclipse and Spring Tool Suite workspace metadata.
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.factorypath
.apt_generated/

# ---- Editor swap and backup files, and the residue `git merge` and
# ---- `git apply` leave behind on a conflict.
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej

# ---- Operating-system metadata. macOS first, then Windows.
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ==================================================================
# THE FROZEN LEGACY CORPUS IS PROTECTED BY OMISSION, NOT BY NEGATION
#
# app/, samples/ and diagrams/ are read-only reference material. app/
# holds the COBOL programs, copybooks, BMS mapsets, JCL, PROC and CTL
# members, the CICS CSD, the VSAM catalogue listing and the fixed-width
# data fixtures; samples/ holds the z/OS compile templates, the build
# procedures and two binary runtime archives; diagrams/ holds the
# legacy illustrations. Every one of those files must survive
# byte-for-byte, and all of them must remain visible.
#
# Unlike .gitattributes and .editorconfig, which close with an explicit
# override block, this file protects those trees by containing no rule
# that can reach them. A `!app/**` line would be indistinguishable from
# real protection while doing nothing at all: a negation only takes
# effect against an earlier pattern that matches, and no pattern above
# matches anything under app/. Adding one would be dead weight of
# exactly the kind that misleads the next reader.
#
# The guarantee is therefore asserted by test rather than by rule, and
# the assertion is the sweep given above:
#
#   git ls-files | git check-ignore --stdin --no-index
#
# It walks every tracked path in the repository and must print nothing.
# Any line of output names a file this configuration would hide from a
# fresh clone, and is a defect in this file. Do not add -v to the
# sweep: it also reports `!` negation matches, so it prints a line for
# .env.example even though .env.example is correctly visible.
# ==================================================================
